From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Playport <dev@playport.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:33:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WOW64: let a 32-bit guest's jump below 64 KiB fault in the
 guest

Madeira's CompileBlock refuses guest RIPs below 0x10000 and returns 0,
which the ARM64EC module turns into the guest's access violation. The
WoW64 module's dispatcher branches to that 0: host pc 0, which Wine
cannot map back to a guest state, so the thread hangs with no guest
exception. Portal 2's engine thread did this after loading localize.dll
(lr at the dispatcher's CompileBlock call, x0 = 0, guest EIP 0).

In the WoW64 module the block compiles as usual: the decoder finds no
executable range at the low address and the guest gets its access
violation, which its own handlers see. The first eight such jumps log
the guest registers ([wow64-low-jump]). Not the guest stack: ESP may
not be readable, and a fault in the compiler would preempt the guest's
access violation.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-inline-translation.md
Offered-upstream: no; the refusal is Madeira's (fex-port 0019)
---
 FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
index 0289ab4..4ffe4d0 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
@@ -1580,11 +1580,34 @@ uintptr_t ContextImpl::CompileBlock(FEXCore::Core::CpuStateFrame* Frame, uint64_
    * NULL-vtable virtual call (`call [rax+8]` with rax=0), control flow
    * lands at RIP=0x8, which then loops compiling thousands of garbage
    * blocks before SEH unwinds. Returning 0 here raises C0000005 to the
-   * guest immediately so the first AV is the only AV. */
+   * guest immediately so the first AV is the only AV.
+   *
+   * Playport: only the ARM64EC module turns that 0 into the guest's fault. The
+   * WoW64 module's dispatcher branches to it: host pc 0, which Wine cannot map
+   * back to the guest, so the thread hangs. There the block compiles as usual:
+   * the decoder finds no executable range and the guest gets its access
+   * violation at the low address. */
+#if !defined(_WIN32) || defined(ARCHITECTURE_arm64ec)
   if (GuestRIP < 0x10000) {
     LogMan::Msg::IFmt("[iOS] CompileBlock: REFUSING low/invalid RIP={:#x}", GuestRIP);
     return 0;
   }
+#else
+  if (GuestRIP < 0x10000) {
+    // Log registers only: the guest stack may be unmapped or unreadable.
+    // Bounded: a guest may retry from its handler.
+    static std::atomic<uint32_t> LowJumps {};
+    if (LowJumps.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) < 8) {
+      const auto& GRegs = Frame->State.gregs;
+      const uint32_t ESP = static_cast<uint32_t>(GRegs[X86State::REG_RSP]);
+      LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[wow64-low-jump] guest RIP={:#x} eax={:#x} ecx={:#x} edx={:#x} ebx={:#x} "
+                        "esp={:#x} ebp={:#x} esi={:#x} edi={:#x}",
+                        GuestRIP, GRegs[X86State::REG_RAX], GRegs[X86State::REG_RCX],
+                        GRegs[X86State::REG_RDX], GRegs[X86State::REG_RBX], ESP, GRegs[X86State::REG_RBP], GRegs[X86State::REG_RSI],
+                        GRegs[X86State::REG_RDI]);
+    }
+  }
+#endif
 
 #ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
